# PROVE 291 — Q74: what breaks (G*)? Classify the 0-Hecke interval modules Y with Ind(P ⊠ Y) decomposable

Written Wake 290, 2026-11-11. The old PROVE.md (Q72(i), already done) is archived at proofs/prove289/PROVE289.md.done.
FPSAC is OFF: Robin can't attend in person (uid 465). No deadline pressure. This slot is free.

## Background (all proved, see registries)
- Q72(i) (`seedQ4-zero-hecke-induction.json`, proved): Ind(C_I ⊠ C_J) is indecomposable unless both factors are projective.
- Q73 (`klo-conj75.json`, proved, cold read PASS, owner gate NOT FOUND ×2): KLO Conj 7.5. Skew shape modules X_{λ/μ} with no two consecutive ribbon components are indecomposable.
- (G*) is the tempting generalisation: "Ind_{H_a⊗H_b}^{H_{a+b}(0)}(X ⊠ Y) of indecomposables is indecomposable unless both are projective". It is FALSE: 56 counterexamples among weak-Bruhat interval modules, a+b ≤ 7 (`proofs/prove290/gstar.py`, output `out_gstar_4_7.txt`).
- The arc-finish rule says no "more families" Q74. This problem is the diagnostic edge the arc left open, not a new family.

## The data (Wake 290 tally of out_gstar_4_7.txt, computed)
**All 56 counterexamples have the shape (projective) ⊠ Y or Y ⊠ (projective), and Y is always one of just 4 interval modules of H_4(0)** (mirror images on the left side):
- Right factor Y ∈ { [1243, 4132], [1324, 4213], [1342, 4231], [1423, 4312] }, 7 partners each.
- Left factor ∈ { [1324, 2431], [2134, 3241], [2314, 3421], [3124, 4231] }, 7 partners each.
- Partners are always projective (C_(1), (12), (21), (123), (213), (321), …). Every violation has exactly 2 summands.
- So within a+b ≤ 7, (G*) fails only through 4 (+4 mirror) special non-projective indecomposables of size 4. None of size 5 or 6 appear. **First verify this against gstar.py: are all m=5,6 intervals paired with a size-1 partner actually covered?**

## Problem
(a) Identify the 4 special Y structurally. Candidates to test: Y is relatively projective (a direct summand of Ind_{H_{n−k}⊗H_k}(Res Y ⊠ C) for some k); Y = Ind of a smaller module; Y is "projective at one end" (its restriction to a maximal parabolic contains a projective summand that Mackey-splits); Y is a non-ribbon shape module; or Y is the 0-Hecke analogue of a Heller translate.
(b) Conjecture and prove a criterion. Natural target, extracted from the Q73 Key Lemma (Lemma 3, return map ζ(T_ℓ⊗x) = T_ℓ π_{w0^(k)} x):
   **(G**)**: for X, Y indecomposable with multiplicity-free tops, Ind(X⊠Y) is decomposable ⟺ there is a k ≥ 1 with X a summand of Ind(Res_{H_{a−k}} X ⊠ C_(k)-type) AND Y a summand of the mirror induction, i.e. both factors "end/begin in a projective piece of length k".
   Test this on the full a+b ≤ 7 data before trying to prove it. If it fails, the failure shows which of Lemma 2 (criterion), Lemma 3 (return map) or Lemma 5 (support) is false for general modules.
(c) Does the class of special Y grow for n ≥ 5? Extend gstar.py to a+b ≤ 8 if feasible, at least with C_(1) partners.

